## ABSTRACT
To develop high-performance metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for catalysis is of great importance. Here, we synthesized the mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3-x}\mathrm{Zn}_{x}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (BTC = benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) nanocubes in a deep eutectic solvent of \(\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}\)/ethylene glycol solution. The route can proceed at room temperature and the reaction time needed is shortened to be \(30\ \mathrm{min}\), which is superior to the conventional solvothermal route that usually needs high temperature and long reaction time. The formation mechanism of the mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3-x}\mathrm{Zn}_{x}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) nanocubes in deep eutectic solvent (DES) was investigated by in situ synchrotron X-ray diffraction/small angle X-ray scattering/X-ray absorption fine structure conjunction technique. The mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3-x}\mathrm{Zn}_{x}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) nanocubes exhibit high catalytic activity and reusability for cyanosilylation reaction of benzaldehyde and aerobic oxidation reaction of benzylic alcohol.
## KEYWORDS
bimetallic metal-organic framework (MOF), mesoporous nanocubes, deep eutectic solvent, formation mechanism, catalytic performance
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an appealing class of crystalline porous materials composed of metal cations/clusters and organic linkers [1], which have been widely used in fields of gas storage and separation [2–4], catalysis [5, 6], sensor [7], theragnosis [8, 9], etc. Particularly, MOFs exhibit catalytic potentials towards many reactions owing to their rich compositions and modifiable structures [10]. The catalytic performances of MOFs are mainly restricted by (1) relatively low activity resulting from the obstruction of active metal sites by organic linkers and resistance of mass transfer [11] and (2) poor stability. Diverse kinds of methods have been proposed to overcome these limitations. One of the mostly adopted route is by introducing a secondary catalytic phase into MOF, but suffering from synthesis complexity and phase separation during catalysis process. Another useful route is through nanoengineering for MOFs, e.g., fabricating low-dimensional nanostructures [6], constructing hierarchically porous structures [12], and designing defects [13]. Compared with MOF-based composite catalyst, the use of single MOF as catalyst is more attractive for the ease in preparation, avoidance of phase separation, and well-controlled performance through structure tailoring, but is challenging.
Solvothermal route is the common method for synthesizing MOFs. The usually used solvents are organic liquids (e.g., N,N
Solvothermal route is the common method for synthesizing MOFs. The usually used solvents are organic liquids (e.g., N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide) or their mixed solvent with ethanol or water. In most cases, high temperature (>100 °C) and long reaction time (couples of days) are required to overcome the high enthalpies of MOF formation or account for the slow kinetics of nucleation [14–16]. Deep eutectic solvent (DES) is an extended class of ionic liquid constructed from H-bond donor and acceptor via hydrogen bonding [17]. For the easy availability from relatively inexpensive components, biodegradability, and nonselectivity [18], DESs have been utilized in various areas such as metallurgy [19], biotransformation [20, 21], and nanomaterial synthesis [22–24]. There are a few reports on using DES as an alternative of conventional solvent for MOF synthesis. For example, choline chloride/glycerol DES was used for synthesizing UiO-66 at 120 °C for 48 h [25]; choline chloride/urea DES was utilized for producing Cu-BDC at 45 °C for 6 h [26] and HKUST-1 at 80 °C for 3 days, [27], respectively. It is interesting to develop MOF catalysts with delicate structures and high performances by fully utilizing the advantages of DES.
Herein, we propose for the first time the utilization of zinc chloride (ZnCl2)/ethylene glycol (EG) DES for the formation of (Cu,Zn)-bimetallic MOF. Interestingly, the route can proceed at room temperature and the reaction time needed is shortened extremely to be 30 min. By the strategy, the mesoporous nanocubes of (Cu,Zn)-bimetallic MOF were obtained. The
formation mechanism for such a novel MOF structure in DES was researched by in situ synchrotron X-ray diffraction/small angle X-ray scattering/X-ray absorption fine structure (XRD/SAXS/XAFS) conjunction technique. Owing to the small particle size, mesoporous structure, and bimetallic feature, the \((\mathrm{Cu},\mathrm{Zn})\)-bimetallic MOF exhibits high activity and stability for the cyanosilylation reaction of benzaldehyde and aerobic oxidation reaction of benzylic alcohol.
## 2 Results and discussion
To get the target product, the desired amount of copper(II) acetate monohydrate and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid was added into the pre-prepared \(Z\mathrm{nCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}\) DES (with \(Z\mathrm{nCl}_{2}\) to EG molar ratio of 1:6). After stirring at room temperature for \(50\,\mathrm{\min}\), the solid product was obtained by washing and drying.
[30, 31]. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and pore volume are \(684.3\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.58\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively (Table S2 in the ESM). The pore size distribution curve, calculated from density functional theory analysis, shows a mesopore size distribution centered at \(\sim17\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(f)), which is consistent with TEM images.
For comparison, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was synthesized by a common solvothermal route in DMF/ethanol/water at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\) [32]. The product presents as solid particles in micron (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Its \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherm exhibits type I mode corresponding to microporous MOF (Fig. 1(e)). The BET surface area and pore volume are \(936.7\;\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.56\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively (Table S2 in the ESM). The decreased BET surface area of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) than \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) can be attributed to the interpenetration of the pores [33]. The micropore volume of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) \((0.19\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) is lower than that of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\), suggesting lowered crystallinity, which is consistent with XRD results.
The insightful chemical and structural information of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Fig. S4 in the ESM). For \(\mathrm{Cu}\) 2p XPS spectrum, the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) region shows two distinct peaks at 935.0 and \(932.9\ \mathrm{eV}\), which are assigned to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\), respectively (Fig. 2(a)). The \(\mathrm{Cu}\) LMM Auger spectrum further proves the presence of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (Fig. 2(b)), which can be ascribed to the linker missing in framework structure along with the formation of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) [6]. For \(Z\mathrm{n}\) 2p XPS spectrum, there is a single \(Z\mathrm{n}\) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak at \(1,022.4\ \mathrm{eV}\), implying that \(Z\mathrm{n}\) exists as \(+2\) valence state in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (Fig. 2(c)) [28, 34]. The \(\mathrm{O}\) 1s XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) presents two curve-fitted peaks at 531.9 and \(533.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zn}-\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) species, respectively (Fig. 2(d)). The relative strength of \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zn}-\mathrm{O}\) to \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) is obviously higher than that of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\), indicating that more \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zn}-\mathrm{O}\) clusters in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) nanocubes [6].
