Second, the evolution process of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) was investigated by varying the synthesis time from 5 to \( 30\ \mathrm{min} \), the other experimental conditions being the same with those above. The products obtained at different reaction times were characterized by TEM. At reaction time of \( 10\ \mathrm{min} \), small MOF cubic nanoparticles come into being (Fig. S5 in the ESM). Then more and more pores emerge inside the nanocubes with prolonged reaction time. The \( \mathrm{Cu/Zn} \) molar ratios of the products synthesized at 10, 15, and \( 20\ \mathrm{min} \) were determined to be 1.9, 3.0, and 4.5, respectively. Simultaneously, the amounts of mesopores increase with processing time, as evidenced by \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption–desorption isotherms (Fig. S6 and Table S3 in the ESM). These results suggest that the hollowness of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) is related with the dissociation of \( \mathrm{Zn} \) ions from the framework. By combination of the above results with in situ XRD/SAXS/XAFE conjunction technique, it can be concluded
that the \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) goes through a rapid crystallization process and then an etching process for mesoporous structure formation.
Third, a control experiment using pure EG (instead of \( \mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG} \) DES) as solvent for \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) was conducted. The reaction time was \( 30\ \mathrm{min} \) and the other experimental conditions were the same with those for \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) synthesis. XRD pattern reveals the formation of crystalline \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Different from the morphology of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \), the product presents particle aggregates without mesopores (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The results prove that \( \mathrm{ZnCl}_{2} \) is dominant for the formation of mesoporous structure of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) nanocubes. Based on all the above results, a possible mechanism for the formation of the mesoporous \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) nanocubes in \( \mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG} \) DES is proposed. In the initial stage, Cu ions and Zn ions co-coordinate with the deprotonated \( \mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC} \) to construct nanosized framework. Since the coordination of Zn ion with deprotonated \( \mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC} \) is weaker than that of Cu ion, the framework retains the \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) crystalline lattice. Then the weakly bonded linker to Zn ions can be dissociated from framework, which could provide copious sites for further etching and afterwards enable the creation of mesopores in \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) nanocubes [38].
Considering that \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) has high pore volume and abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes, it was used as a Lewis acid catalyst for the cyanosilylation of aldehydes to the corresponding cyanohydrins, which is a class of important organic intermediates for both biological processes and synthetic chemistry [39]. The reaction was carried out at \( 60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) under solvent-free condition [40]. The product was determined by \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR). For comparison, the catalytic performance of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) for the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde was also studied at the same experimental conditions. Remarkably, there is a significant increase in benzaldehyde conversion as catalyzed by \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) compared with \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) (Fig. 4(a)). At \( 8\ \mathrm{h} \), benzaldehyde can be converted completely to 2-phenyl-2-[(trimethylsilyl)oxy] acetonitrile by \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \).
By contrast, the conversion of benzaldehyde is only \(16\%\) catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\). It is worth noting that the catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) is much better than those of the reported \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) under similar reaction conditions (Table S4 in the ESM).
After reaction for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\), the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was recovered and its reusability for the reaction was tested. The conversion of benzaldehyde remains unchanged after the \(4^{\mathrm{th}}\) run for catalysis (Fig. 4(b)). No obvious difference was observed for the XRD patterns, TEM images, and XPS spectra of the fresh and reused \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM), indicating the well preserved structural integrity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) catalyst. ICP-AES was used to determine whether there were metal ions leached to filtrate after reaction for four runs. No copper ions or zinc ions were detected, further confirming the stability of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) during catalysis. By contrast, when \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was used as catalyst, \(30\%\) yield was lost for the third run. XRD pattern of the reused \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) catalyst reveals that the framework structure was destroyed (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
\(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was then used for catalysis of the cyanosilylation reactions of a series of para-substituted aromatic aldehydes. The reaction time was fixed at \(8\mathrm{~h~}\), while the other experimental conditions were the same with above. As shown in Fig. 4(c), the catalytic performances of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) for the cyanosilylation reactions of all substrates are much higher than those of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\). Moreover, the aldehydes with an electron
withdrawing group attached to the phenyl ring (–F, conversion \(97\%\); \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), conversion \(94\%\)) are much more reactive than those with an electron-donating group \(\left(-\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right.\) conversion \(73\%\)).
Moreover, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) catalyst was used for the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol. The results show that \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) catalysts are much more active and stable than \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (Figs. S11–S14 and Table S5 in the ESM).
The high activity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) can be mainly ascribed to its hierarchically micro-mesoporous and nanosized structure [41, 42]. Such a structure results in the existence of abundant unsaturated coordination \(\mathrm{Cu}\) sites on surfaces, which could act as Lewis acid sites to bind with the \(\mathrm{O}\) atoms of the aldehydes to activate the carbonyl \(\mathrm{C}\) atoms followed by reaction with trimethylsilyl cyanide [39, 40]. Moreover, the mesoporous structure provides more accessible active sites, improves substrate adsorption, and facilitates mass diffusion during reaction [41]. The \(\mathrm{Zn}\) nodes not only induce the mesopore formation of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) for improving catalyst activity, but play a role of enhancing catalyst stability [43–46]. The \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) catalyst keeps its crystal structure after four runs, while the framework of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) is destroyed after three catalytic runs. It indicates that the \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) ions are efficient in preserving the stability of framework during catalytic process, similar to the results reported in Ref. [43].
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we synthesized mesoporous \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) nanocubes in \(\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}\) DES. This strategy produces bimetallic MOF with small particle size \((\sim100\ \mathrm{nm})\), hierarchical micro- and mesopores, and abundant unsaturated coordination metal sites. The as-synthesized \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) nanocubes have shown high efficiency and reusability for the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde and the aerobic oxidation of benzylic alcohol. The proposed DES route for MOF synthesis is facile, rapid, and environmentally benign and can proceed at room-temperature. We anticipate that it could be applied to the fabrication of other MOFs and MOF-based materials with desirable compositions and functions.
